Limits on what one peer can make ENet hold or do, and reads kept inside what arrived.

Our own patch (AvP), from our review of ENet 1.3.18 as a reader of hostile packets
(cards ENET-E1 to ENET-E4 in our notes). In order of how much each matters:

- A held message counts its own records, not only its bytes, against the limit on
  what is waiting (maximumWaitingData). Messages with no bytes counted nothing, so a
  peer that skipped one number could make ENet hold millions of them.
- A message that comes in pieces starts cleared. It counts as complete once every
  piece number has arrived, whatever bytes the pieces covered, so the rest of it was
  whatever was in memory before.
- A packet is read up to ENET_PROTOCOL_MAXIMUM_PACKET_COMMANDS commands, the most an
  honest ENet sends. One packet could hold a thousand commands, each asking for an
  answer.
- A packet shorter than its own header is dropped, a command is copied only as far
  as its own size, and a command number past the known kinds has no size, so nothing
  is read past what arrived or past the end of a list.

Honest traffic between two copies of this ENet is unchanged.

